  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 5.1. Increased capacity of the ILO constituents to identify current skills mismatches and anticipate future skill needs</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">In the period of January 2020 to July 2021, the Commission for Technical, and Vocational Education and Training (CTVET), using ILO&#x2019;s STED (Skills for Trade and Economic Diversification) method, established Sectoral Skills Councils (SSCs) in the following 5 selected sectors: Agriculture, Tourism and Hospitality, Construction, Oil and Gas and Information and Communication Technologies. These tripartite structures play an important role in enabling the Technical Vocational Education and Training system (TVET) to provide training that effectively responds to the labour market skills needs. The ILO facilitated the training on its STED method and provided technical advice to the establishment of Sector Skills Councils in the Tourism and Hospitality and Oil and Gas sectors from January to December 2020. It also supported the establishment of Sector Skills Councils in the Construction, Agriculture and Information and Communication Technologies from January 2020 to July 2021. The ILO supported the development of a National Framework on the structure and function of SSBs, and piloted its implementation in the 5 established SSCs, from January to July 2021. The ILO provided technical advisory services to the development of skills strategies and action plans for the 5 established SSCs. The ILO supported the assessment of the inclusion of youth with disabilities in TVET and among selected companies in the Ghanaian Tourism and Hospitality Sector (June 2020); 8 members of the Tourism and Hospitality Sector Skills Body benefited from training on Social Inclusion hosted by ICT-ILO from June to August 2020. Fifty stakeholders in the TVET sector benefited from training on ILO&#x2019;s Quality Apprenticeship model hosted by ICT-ILO from August - September 2020.

Ten CTVET instructors from selected TVET institutions benefited  from training on the digitization of TVET hosted by ITC-ILO (Module 1 &#x2013; Introduction from July  2020 and Module 2 &#x2013; Construction of an e-learning environment from October  2020. The ILO supported the development a career Pathway Map for the Tourism and Hospitality sector in August 2020. It also supported the capacity building in leadership management for members of the Agriculture, Construction and Tourism &amp; Hospitality SSBs in June 2021.  ILO assisted in the conduct of a situation analysis and developed a greening strategy to promote sustainable practices in TVET and its action plan, from March to July 2021.</narrative>
   </description>
